{"reference":"Matthew 17","book":"Matthew","chapter":17,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"A Distinct Tax Question","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The payment discussed in verses 24–27 is a temple-associated tax scene, not the later controversy about tribute to Caesar. Matthew therefore places these questions within different fiscal and political settings; they should not be read as the same issue.","verse_refs":["Matthew 17:24-27"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_matthew_017_8fd6d08597ac7b7ff8847a73"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"why_it_matters","title":"Why the Distinction Matters","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The Gospel accounts use different settings and vocabulary for imperial tribute, toll-related work, and temple-associated payments. Keeping those distinctions in view prevents these passages from being flattened into a single, generic discussion of taxes.","verse_refs":["Matthew 17:24-27"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_matthew_017_12a6ea73d710f616d15299bc"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}